lin: Quick triage - generator sample in examples/go seems to leak goroutines when callers cancel the parent context while auth refresh/backoff is happening. Small repro inlined.

rahul: Paste the repro? I want to run it with a slow auth proxy.

lin: Trimmed from examples:```go
func Run(ctx context.Context, c *redwood.Client) error {
    it := c.Generator().Stream(ctx, &redwood.GenParams{Prompt: "hi"})
    for it.Next() {
        _ = it.Token()
        if ctx.Err() != nil {
            break
        }
    }
    // Close() sometimes blocks because the internal read loop is stuck on backoff
    return it.Close()
}
```

emily: Confirmed locally with auth refresh delayed - saw ~15 goroutines in streamIterator.readLoop after ctx cancel. Close() blocked.

rahul: Two options: add Close(ctx) so callers can set a deadline, or expose Abort() that force-closes without waiting. Close(ctx) is more idiomatic and keeps compatibility if we keep Close() shim.

lin: Proposed shim: `func (it *Iter) Close() error { return it.CloseContext(context.Background()) }` + `func (it *Iter) CloseContext(ctx context.Context) error` which cancels internal pump and waits bounded by ctx. Also change internal read loop to select on an internal cancel channel so shutdown is immediate.

emily: +1. Also audit auth retry/backoff to ensure it checks the iterator-level cancel channel during sleeps. Otherwise CloseContext won't help much.

lin: I'll open a tiny PR with tests that simulate slow auth and assert no goroutine leak after CloseContext with timeout. Will update examples/go/README to mention CloseContext when cancelling.

